ss node by node, we can maintain a multiset or set of symbols present. But note that the subtree might contain multiple occurrences of a symbol; we need to ensure that at least one remains. So we can maintain a dictionary mapping symbol to count of nodes with that symbol in retained set. Before removing a node, we compute the counts in its subtree, then check if for any required symbol, the after-removal count would be >=1 (i.e., current count - subtree_count >= 1). If yes, removal allowed.\n\nWe also need to handle non-removable nodes: they cannot be removed regardless.\n\nNow, the order of processing: sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (string) for tie-break. That ensures deeper first.\n\nReturn removed_node_ids in the order they were removed (i.e., the order we added to removed_set). Retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably alphabetical? We'll sort them as strings).\n\nWe must also consider that root is depth 0, parent null, removable false. Root symbol is \"root\". Required_symbols may include \"root\". We never remove root.\n\nEdge cases: If a node is removable but its subtree contains a required symbol that appears only in that subtree, then removal would break requirement, so we skip.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Build a dict mapping node id to node dict.\n2. Build a dict mapping node id to list of child ids (by parent field). Also track root id (parent null).\n3. Validate payload: required fields exist.\n4. Initialize removed_set = set().\n5. Compute initial symbol counts: for each node not removed, count symbol. But initially all nodes retained.\n6. Sort nodes by depth desc, then id asc.\n7. For each node in that order:\n   - If node id in removed_set: skip (should not happen, but safety).\n   - If not node['removable']: skip.\n   - If node['symbol'] in required_symbols: skip (cannot remove a node whose symbol is required).\n   - If node['id'] == root: skip (root is not removable anyway, but double-check).\n   - Check children: get