nodes that are not already removed. And update symbol counts accordingly. But since we are processing deeper first, it's likely that no descendant is still retained if they were removable? But if a descendant was non-removable or required symbol, it would be retained and thus block parent removal. So if parent removal is allowed, all descendants must be either already removed (so they are in removed_set) or they are retained but removal of parent would remove them; but if they are retained and removal would remove them, then the requirement check must account for that. Actually when we compute subtree_counts, we only include nodes that are not already removed. That is the set of nodes that would be newly removed by this operation. So after removal, we add all those nodes to removed_set. And we update symbol counts accordingly.\n   - Else: skip.\n\n8. After processing all nodes, compute retained_node_ids: all node ids not in removed_set. Sort them alphabetically (as strings).\n9. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nImportant: The algorithm must preserve stable tie-break: at equal depth, use node ID ascending. We'll sort nodes accordingly.\n\nAlso note: The case payload may have \"_case_nonce\" which we ignore.\n\nNow, implement solve(case) accordingly. Use only standard library.\n\nWe'll write the Python code and then wrap in JSON with key \"implementation\".\n\nMake sure to handle potential cyclic references? Should not.\n\nAlso, we must validate the case structure: ensure nodes list and required_symbols present.\n\nEdge case: If a node's parent is null, it's root. Root should have id \"root\" but not necessarily? In given examples it is \"root\". We'll treat any node with parent null as root, and ensure not removed.\n\nAlso, note that nodes may have \"parent\" that refers to an id that doesn't exist? Not in valid inputs.\n\nWe'll implement.\n\nNow, produce the final JSON with key \"implementation\". The valu